Tiny C Compiler - C Scripting Everywhere - The Smallest ANSI C compiler
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

Features:
--------

- SMALL! You can compile and execute C code everywhere, for example on
  rescue disks.

- FAST! tcc generates machine code for i386, x86_64, arm, aarch64 or
  riscv64.  Compiles and links about 10 times faster than 'gcc -O0'.

- UNLIMITED! Any C dynamic library can be used directly. TCC is
  heading toward full ISOC99 compliance. TCC can of course compile
  itself.

- SAFE! tcc includes an optional memory and bound checker. Bound
  checked code can be mixed freely with standard code.

- Compile and execute C source directly. No linking or assembly
  necessary. Full C preprocessor included.

- C script supported : just add '#!/usr/local/bin/tcc -run' at the first
  line of your C source, and execute it directly from the command
  line.

Documentation:
-------------

1) Build and installation (Zig 0.16.0)

   zig build
   zig build test
   zig build run -- -run examples/ex1.c

Zig is the only build tool required. It compiles the C sources, generates
configuration and builtin headers, and bootstraps TinyCC's runtime. No
Make, configure, external C compiler, archiver, Python or Perl is needed.
Native Unix builds require the platform's libc development headers;
macOS requires the Command Line Tools SDK (automatically found via xcrun).
Windows uses the headers and import definitions bundled in this repository.

The default output is zig-out/:
   bin/tcc                 compiler (tcc.exe on Windows)
   lib/libtcc.a            embedding library (lib/tcc.lib on Windows)
   include/libtcc.h        public embedding API
   lib/tcc/                runtime objects, library and TinyCC headers

   zig build -Doptimize=ReleaseFast
   zig build -Dshared=true
   zig build --prefix /your/install/prefix
   zig build --help

Building also installs into the selected prefix. The prefix is compiled
into TinyCC; after moving the installation, pass -B/path/to/lib/tcc.
Shared builds install libtcc.so, libtcc.dylib or tcc.dll as appropriate.
The source manual and license are installed in share/doc/tcc. Rendering
Texinfo is not part of the build, so documentation tools are not required.

Use -Dtarget for the platform on which the compiler will run, and
-Dtcc-target to override the platform for which it generates code:

   zig build -Dtarget=x86_64-windows-gnu --prefix zig-out/windows
   zig build -Dtcc-target=x86_64-linux-gnu -Dsysroot=/path/to/sysroot

Supported CPU backends: x86, x86_64, arm, aarch64, riscv64. OS targets:
Linux, macOS (x86_64/aarch64), Windows and FreeBSD/NetBSD/OpenBSD.
Cross builds use a host TinyCC bootstrap to generate the target runtime;
Unix target runtime builds require target libc headers via -Dsysroot.
Use -Druntime=false to build just the compiler and embedding library.
Run tests on a native target. Foreign binaries require an appropriate host.

Other options: -Dbacktrace=false, -Dbcheck=false, -Ddwarf=4, -Dtriplet=...,
-Dtccdir=..., -Dsysinclude=..., -Dlibpaths=..., -Dcrtprefix=...,
-Delfinterp=.... Search paths use ':' on Unix and ';' on Windows.

Tests are driven by Zig and run in temporary cache directories:

   zig build test                  regression, preprocessor, API, ABI, bootstrap
   zig build tests2                compiler regression tests
   zig build test-pp               preprocessor tests
   zig build tests2 -Dtest-filter=139_

Existing platform exclusions are preserved and counted as skipped tests.
The bootstrap test compares three TinyCC generations with Zig's C compiler.
Memory debugging, bounds checking and DLL self-hosting are also exercised.
The standalone gcctestsuite.sh and arm-asm-testsuite.sh scripts are optional
external-tool comparisons, and are not dependencies of any Zig build step.
Build products stay in .zig-cache/ and zig-out/; remove these directories
for a completely clean build.


2) Introduction

We assume here that you know ANSI C. Look at the example ex1.c to know
what the programs look like.

The include file <tcclib.h> can be used if you want a small basic libc
include support (especially useful for floppy disks). Of course, you
can also use standard headers, although they are slower to compile.

You can begin your C script with '#!/usr/local/bin/tcc -run' on the first
line and set its execute bits (chmod a+x your_script). Then, you can
launch the C code as a shell or perl script :-) The command line
arguments are put in 'argc' and 'argv' of the main functions, as in
ANSI C.

3) Examples

ex1.c: simplest example (hello world). Can also be launched directly
as a script: './ex1.c'.

ex2.c: more complicated example: find a number with the four
operations given a list of numbers (benchmark).

ex3.c: compute fibonacci numbers (benchmark).

ex4.c: more complicated: X11 program. Very complicated test in fact
because standard headers are being used ! As for ex1.c, can also be launched
directly as a script: './ex4.c'.

ex5.c: 'hello world' with standard glibc headers.

tcc.c: TCC can of course compile itself. Used to check the code
generator.

tcctest.c: auto test for TCC which tests many subtle possible bugs. Used
when doing 'zig build test'.

4) Full Documentation

Please read tcc-doc.texi for all the features of TCC. Additional
information for the Windows port is in tcc-win32.txt.

